JavaScript LRU cache with a recency bug that only shows under a specific access pattern. Save lru.js exactly as given, ES module:

export class LRU {
  constructor(cap) { this.cap = cap; this.m = new Map(); }
  get(k) {
    return this.m.has(k) ? this.m.get(k) : -1;
  }
  put(k, v) {
    if (this.m.has(k)) this.m.delete(k);
    this.m.set(k, v);
    if (this.m.size > this.cap) {
      this.m.delete(this.m.keys().next().value);
    }
  }
}

A Map iterates in insertion order, so eviction targets the oldest INSERTED key — but get() never refreshes a key's position, so a recently-read key gets evicted. Write lru.test.js with node:test: first a failing test for the pattern put(1), put(2), get(1), put(3) on capacity 2 — which key must survive, and which does the buggy code actually keep? Run `node --test`, show the failure. Fix get() to refresh recency (delete + re-set), then extend tests: overwrite updates value AND recency, capacity 1, a 2000-op seeded fuzz against a reference implementation using an array for recency ordering. Finish with the full test run green.

