content.text:
All green. Summary:

**The bug pattern:** `put(1)`, `put(2)`, `get(1)`, `put(3)` on capacity 2 — key 1 must survive (most recently *used*), but the buggy `get()` returned the value without touching Map insertion order, so eviction removed **key 1** and kept stale key 2. The failing run showed `get(1)` returning `-1` instead of `'a'`.

**Files:**
- `lru.js` — fixed `get()` does delete + re-set, moving the key to the MRU position:
```js
get(k) {
  if (!this.m.has(k)) return -1;
  const v = this.m.get(k);
  this.m.delete(k);   // refresh recency
  this.m.set(k, v);
  return v;
}
```
- `lru.test.js` — 4 tests:
  1. The original recency-on-get failure case
  2. Overwrite updates value *and* recency (`put` of an existing key must also refresh order, since it goes through delete+set)
  3. Capacity-1 edge cases (eviction on every insert, overwrite, get-on-sole-key)
  4. A 2000-op seeded fuzz (mulberry32 PRNG, seed `0xdeadbeef`) comparing against a reference LRU that tracks recency with an explicit array — keys 0–11 over capacity 7 force heavy churn, with full 12-key agreement probes every 100 ops

One note: my first draft of test 2 itself had the wrong expectation (I forgot the intermediate `get(1)` call refreshes recency and changes what `put(4)` evicts) — worth remembering when testing LRU logic: every `get` in your test is a state mutation.
